WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.400
What happens when you throw a bunch of&nbsp;
transforming cars, transforming animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.700
and a planet-eating world-ender into the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.480
movie? A whole lot of confusing&nbsp;
moments. Let's dig into them.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:18.660
"Transformers: Rise of the Beasts" opens with&nbsp;
a scene set on the Maximals' home planet. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:24.660
introduced to Optimus Primal and his gang, and we&nbsp;
also meet the vicious Terrorcons. The villains are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:30.120
sent by the malicious planet-sized Transformer&nbsp;
Unicron, who desires the Maximals' powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:36.060
Transwarp Key. His plan? To use its space-time&nbsp;
portal powers to journey across the universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.720
and feed on even more planets.
Unfortunately for Unicron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:45.240
his plans are thwarted. Now trapped in&nbsp;
his current galaxy, Unicron sends Scourge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:50.100
out into the cosmos to track it down for him.
The big question here that might come to mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:56.640
is why Scourge and the Terrorcons can transverse&nbsp;
the universe without the Transwarp Key but Unicron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.760
can't. He's supposedly more powerful than all of&nbsp;
them combined. Couldn't he theoretically visit and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.580
feed on any planet Scourge searches for the Key?&nbsp;
"Rise of the Beasts" doesn't directly address this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.980
quandary, but the most likely explanation is that&nbsp;
Unicron's power is more limited than it seems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:19.260
Perhaps moving through space requires too much&nbsp;
energy without guaranteed energon awaiting him.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:24.840
When we first meet Noah Diaz in "Transformers:&nbsp;
Rise of the Beasts," he's en route to interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:30.120
for a security job. However, his interview is&nbsp;
canceled after the man in charge checks Noah's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.860
military record and speaks with his former CO,&nbsp;
who claims that he was unreliable in the Army.&nbsp;

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:40.080
Noah protests, claiming that he had a lot going&nbsp;
on at home because of his little brother's health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.300
condition. However, we don't ever get any more&nbsp;
explanation than that. Did he go AWOL to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:50.820
Chris at some point? You'd think that if he did,&nbsp;
his old CO would have had a lot more bad to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:56.760
about him. So…what then? Was he just distracted?&nbsp;
Always calling home to check in on his brother?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.620
It's hard to imagine how such behavior would&nbsp;
stick in a commander's mind so negatively.&nbsp;

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:07.080
Regardless, Noah's Army experience clearly comes&nbsp;
in handy. He handles himself impressively well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.880
during the final battle, and his tech savviness&nbsp;
is a big boon to his new robotic friends.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:18.660
Noah gets swept up into the Autobots' crusade&nbsp;
when he tries his hand at grand theft auto and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:23.640
unintentionally picks Mirage as the car to&nbsp;
steal. It's actually Noah's friend Reek who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.000
chooses the target, and if you stopped&nbsp;
to think about the robbery, it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.440
really make all that much sense.
From the dialogue we do get, it seems that Reek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.480
was tipped off by another contact that the Porsche&nbsp;
was ripe for the picking. But as Noah enters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:42.360
the garage to jack it, he passes by a number of&nbsp;
equally fancy cars. Why are they targeting Mirage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.800
specifically? It's never really explained,&nbsp;
but there are a few possible explanations.&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.900
First and foremost, the Porsche is probably&nbsp;
the fastest car in the garage and therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.160
the easiest one to get away in. Reek also has&nbsp;
a line about how long it's been sitting there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.740
which could indicate that no&nbsp;
one would miss it for some time.&nbsp;

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:04.860
Last and most fun, it could be destiny. If you're&nbsp;
a longtime "Transformers" fan, you'll know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:10.920
Optimus Prime and crew put a lot of faith in fate&nbsp;
and divine duty. Maybe Noah was simply called to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.240
be exactly where he was supposed to go.
"I'm here because I got in a car."

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:21.360
The timeline of the "Transformers" movies&nbsp;
is, in a word, messy. "Bumblebee" and "Rise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.460
of the Beasts" ostensibly take place in the&nbsp;
same continuity as the Michael Bay films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.440
but there are a lot of plot holes that confuse&nbsp;
that chronology. In "Rise of the Beasts," the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.360
Autobots are stranded on Earth following the&nbsp;
events of "Bumblebee," and Optimus Prime blames&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:41.760
himself. The discovery of the Transwarp Key gives&nbsp;
him hope that they can return home to Cybertron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.080
but that plan ultimately falls through.
There are a few confusing things here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.940
For starters, the Autobots arrive on&nbsp;
Earth from space during the 2007 film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.980
so if all seven movies are indeed in the same&nbsp;
timeline, they'd have to leave and return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:02.820
somewhere between 1994 and then. Second, Optimus'&nbsp;
desire to return home, while understandable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:07.260
seems a bit strange. The whole reason they&nbsp;
left Cybertron in the first place is because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:12.660
they lost the war to the Decepticons. Going home&nbsp;
would surely be dangerous, as there's no reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:18.060
to think their enemies wouldn't still be there.
The simple explanation for these discrepancies is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:23.640
well, plot holes. But that's not too satisfying,&nbsp;
so what else could explain it? We know that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:29.520
Transwarp Key allows for travel through both space&nbsp;
and time, so maybe Optimus wants to go back to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:34.500
earlier version of Cybertron before the war.&nbsp;
That still doesn't explain them all being gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:40.380
at the start of the original "Transformers," but&nbsp;
hey, continuities were made to be broken, right?

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:45.960
Noah, Elena, and the Autobots travel to Peru&nbsp;
in search of the other half of the Transwarp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:50.640
Key. After discovering that it's no longer&nbsp;
in its original resting place, the group is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.960
attacked by Scourge and the Terrorcons. They&nbsp;
battle it out on a winding mountain road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.820
but the fight is disrupted by Airazor,&nbsp;
who destroys the bridge between Optimus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:05.400
and Scourge to allow the Autobots to escape.
Your first time through, it might seem like a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:10.440
of a plot hole that Scourge doesn't immediately&nbsp;
just jump the gap and continue chasing Optimus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:15.600
After all, he's a giant robot, so a little&nbsp;
fire surely isn't enough to scare him off.&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.100
In retrospect, though, it's pretty obvious&nbsp;
that Scourge lets the Autobots get away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:24.180
Since they don't have the Key, he has&nbsp;
no reason to kill them yet. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:29.220
his plan is to corrupt Airazor and use her&nbsp;
as a beacon so that he can track the group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:33.900
down to the Key's real location. And to&nbsp;
his credit, that plan works perfectly.&nbsp;

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:38.700
What's more confusing is why Airazor doesn't&nbsp;
do something about her infection before it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.680
consumes her. When Elena asks about her&nbsp;
injury, Airazor says that Scourge "leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.300
a mark" when he hurts you, suggesting that she&nbsp;
understands the nature of the wound. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.280
she simply waits until the corruption takes hold&nbsp;
of her mind, thus jeopardizing the entire mission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.960
Perhaps the infection begins influencing her&nbsp;
behavior the second it hit her in the sky.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:03.720
Perhaps the most confusing thing in all of&nbsp;
"Transformers: Rise of the Beasts" is the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:09.060
chronology of the Maximals and the Autobots.&nbsp;
The Maximals are hunted by Unicron to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:14.460
home planet, which the evil god quickly devours as&nbsp;
they make their escape. Using the Transwarp Key,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:20.460
they arrive on Earth and befriend a human tribe&nbsp;
in Peru. There they remain in hiding until 1994.&nbsp;

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:26.640
But then we learn that Optimus Primal is named&nbsp;
after Optimus Prime, meaning that the Maximals are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.820
successors, not predecessors, to the Autobots. How&nbsp;
does that work? Well, it isn't exactly explained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:37.920
but in essence, it means that the Maximals&nbsp;
traveled back through time. Since the Transwarp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.600
Key is said to possess time-travel capabilities,&nbsp;
it makes sense that their voyage to Earth could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:47.280
have also launched them deep into the past.
"I am a Maximal, a warrior from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.580
both your past and future."
This also fits with the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:56.640
of the original "Beast Wars: Transformers" cartoon&nbsp;
and toy line. In that timeline, the Maximals were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.260
descendants of the Autobots who journeyed&nbsp;
from Cybertron to a prehistoric Earth via&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:07.320
transwarp travel in pursuit of the evil Predacons.
There are still some confusing bits, however. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:13.800
were Scourge and the Terrorcons able to travel&nbsp;
from the future to 1994 Earth without a Transwarp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:18.660
Key of their own? And does all this temporal&nbsp;
meddling mean that we're now officially in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:23.640
different timeline than the Michael Bay films,&nbsp;
thus explaining some of the other plot holes?

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:28.320
The first big battle in "Transformers: Rise&nbsp;
of the Beasts" takes place at night around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:33.180
the museum where Elena works. The Autobots&nbsp;
face off against the Terrorcons and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:37.560
their metallic rears handed to them,&nbsp;
with Bumblebee even temporarily biting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:41.160
the dust. Despite all the explosions&nbsp;
and the length of the fight, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:45.780
no one seems to notice. This is a recurring&nbsp;
issue in the film, and unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:50.460
not every instance can be explained away.
Why doesn't anyone else wake up and see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:55.440
Mirage at Chris and Noah's window? This is the&nbsp;
Big Apple, after all -- the City that Never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:59.760
Sleeps. It's one of those confusing moments that&nbsp;
we probably just aren't supposed to think about.&nbsp;

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:05.160
While we're at it, it's equally curious that no&nbsp;
one seems to notice the portal opened by Elena's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:09.840
half of the Transwarp Key. The device seems to&nbsp;
be activated by her museum scanner technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:15.600
but its energy apparently can't be seen by humans,&nbsp;
and it shuts off as soon as the Terrorcons attack.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:20.160
If you've seen any of the Michael Bay&nbsp;
"Transformers" movies, you were probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:25.200
confused after seeing Bumblebee get brutally&nbsp;
murdered by Scourge in "Rise of the Beasts."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:31.620
You also probably saw his final-act resurrection&nbsp;
coming a mile away. Optimus Primal shows Optimus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:36.300
Prime a bed of rich, latent energon near&nbsp;
the human village where the Maximals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:41.220
live. They rest Bumblebee there, but Primal&nbsp;
explains that since the energon isn't active,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:46.740
it won't be enough to revive him. Only a massive&nbsp;
amount of energy could energize the energon.&nbsp;

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:52.260
Of course, that's what ends up happening. After&nbsp;
Scourge uses the Transwarp Key to open a portal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:56.280
Unicron begins to pass through into&nbsp;
Earth's atmosphere. As he does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:01.920
he blasts the planet with some kind of scanning&nbsp;
ray, presumably to prepare the energon for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.540
consumption. This move has the unintended&nbsp;
side effect of juicing Bee back to life.&nbsp;

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:12.000
This chain of events poses some other questions&nbsp;
about how "life" for a Transformer actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:17.160
works. We know that every Cybertronian&nbsp;
has a spark powered in part by energon.&nbsp;

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:23.340
"We call it a spark. It contains&nbsp;
our life force and our memories."&nbsp;

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:27.480
"I would call it a soul."
But if one of them can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:31.080
fully dead for days and still get&nbsp;
revived, anything seems possible.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:37.020
During the final battle of "Transformers: Rise&nbsp;
of the Beasts," Mirage tries to take Scourge on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:43.200
by himself to allow Noah and Elena to get to the&nbsp;
Key. Mirage fights valiantly but ultimately loses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:49.260
sacrificing himself in the end to protect Noah.&nbsp;
Mirage takes blast after blast from Scourge's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:54.240
cannon until it seems that all the life has&nbsp;
drained out of him. But in a strange twist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:59.400
he has just enough power left to turn&nbsp;
himself into an exosuit for Noah to pilot.&nbsp;

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:03.840
Is it very cool? Yes. Does every&nbsp;
kid who loves Transformers want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.340
exactly that thing to happen to them?&nbsp;
Hell yeah. But how does it actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:13.740
you know...work? Mirage isn't quite dead,&nbsp;
because he's fixed later in the "Rise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:18.300
the Beasts" mid-credits scene without&nbsp;
the use of powered-up energon. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:23.040
he clearly doesn't have enough strength to do&nbsp;
much on his own. The film foreshadows that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:27.660
can turn himself into a weapon for humans when&nbsp;
he gives Noah a piece of himself as a blaster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:33.000
When you think about it, his Power Rangers&nbsp;
form is just a more dramatic version of that.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:39.060
Okay, let's talk about that thing that happens&nbsp;
at the end. After helping the Autobots and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:44.160
Maximals save Earth from Unicron, Noah returns&nbsp;
to Brooklyn and goes for another security job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:49.020
interview. This time, however, things aren't&nbsp;
what they seem. The man who interviews him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:55.320
Agent Burke, is actually an operative and&nbsp;
recruiter for G.I. Joe. Yes, that G.I. Joe.&nbsp;

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.540
"Yoooo Joe!"
The implication seems clear:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:06.180
Paramount and Hasbro are intending to finally make&nbsp;
a "Transformers" and "G.I. Joe" crossover movie.&nbsp;

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:10.500
Talks of a crossover -- something that has&nbsp;
happened numerous times in the animated series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:15.960
and comics -- have circulated for years.&nbsp;
However, now seems like a weird time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:21.660
make good on them. The last attempt at rebooting&nbsp;
the "G.I. Joe" film franchise was 2021's "Snake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:26.580
Eyes," which received poor reviews overall.&nbsp;
The Michael Bay "Transformers" movies also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:32.220
assert that humans and Autobots didn't form&nbsp;
a proper relationship until the 2010s or so.&nbsp;

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:37.200
So what does all this mean? We'll have&nbsp;
to wait and see. But with multiverses and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:41.880
crossovers all the rage in Hollywood right&nbsp;
now, there's no doubt that Paramount can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:45.480
find a way to throw these two huge&nbsp;
brands together on the big screen.

